A||N||7||jlhuber||#4 cyl||||5||
Z||000000||jlhuber||01-28-2000||11:56 PM||||this may sound like a stupid question to some, but oh well.  what is the easiest way to get the motor into the #4 cyl firing position?  i have misplaced my repair manual and need to know so i can get the car back running how it is meant to.||||reg||5
Z||000001||la9||01-29-2000||01:38 AM||||Is it a V6 ? If so, When the dot on the crank gear is at the top then #1 and #4 are at TDC. When the cam gear dot is at the bottom and lined up with the crank gear then it is at #4 TDC. If the timing mark on the cam is at the top then the engine is at #1 TDC.||||reg||1
Z||000002||jlhuber||01-29-2000||11:51 AM||||'nuff said.  i honestly didn't think it was going to be that easy.  but i know how to do it now, thankyou very much for your reply||||reg||14
Z||000003||daved||01-29-2000||12:14 PM||||You can get close by removing the plug, putting a finger or thumb over the plug hole, and have someone turn the engine with a wrench on the bolt in the center of the crankshaft pulley.  You feel the pressure from the cylinder only as the piston comes up on its compression stroke. I,ve used a plastic probe onto the top of the piston to determine when the piston is at TDC. Easy for #1, or for you folks with 4 cyl. engine, but on the V6 #4, the hole is not easy to access against the firewall, and my trick may not work.<BR>Why not just use the timing mark on #1?<BR>Hope this helps, Dave||||reg||1
Z||000004||jlhuber||01-29-2000||09:45 PM||||oh yeah, which one is the #4 cyl?  i have the v-6||||reg||1
Z||000005||la9||01-29-2000||10:08 PM||||Cylinders on the rear of the car are #1 #3 #5 and the ones toward the passenger compartment are #2 #4 #6 so #4 cylinder is the middle cylinder closest to the passenger compartment. Hope that helps.||||reg||1
Z||000006||cobra427||01-30-2000||07:44 PM||||Ia9,<BR>my turn to ask a dumb question. Is the cam <BR>gear marked on thos dots as being #1 or #4?<BR>If not how do you tell if you have the cam gear right and not 180* out of sync.||||reg||1
Z||000007||la9||01-30-2000||08:02 PM||||There is just one dot on each gear. You line the two dots up (cam gear bottom, crank gear top) then when you turn it to the top #1 is TDC. I was worried about the 180 degree problem myself but according to the machine shop and the pontiac manual this is how it is done. #1 and #4 are at TDC at the same time so when the marks are lined up at the bottom the #1 cylinder is on the compression stroke then when the cam mark is at the top #1 cylinder fires and #4 is on the compression stroke. Remember the crank gear turns twice each time the cam gear does. Email me if I'm not expaining myself clear enough and then when we come up with a better explaination I'll post it in the forum.<p>[This message has been edited by la9 (edited 01-30-2000).]||||reg||1
